Goals
* Implement a reverse proxy to centralize access to internal Docker services.
* Introduce HTTPS encrpytion for internal applications. 
* Prepare my lab for identity-based access control.
* Maintain full isolation from my home network. 

Tools
* Raspberry Pi 5
* Docker
* Portainer
* Nginx
* Self signed certiface

What I Did
* Created a dedicated Docker network to allow internal container communication
* Deployed Nginx Proxy Manager using Docker Compose. 
* Mapped out and configured ports to avoid conflict. 
* Attatched Portainer to my proxy network. 
* Created a self signed certifiafe using openSSL. 

Why
* Used a reverse proxy to create a single controlled entry point to internal services.
* Avoided direct IP:port access patterns and moved toward centralized routing. 
* Implemented TLS terminatino at the proxy layer to simulate enterpise load balancing. 
* Kept all changed isolated to Docker network without modifying my home netowrk. 
* Built the foudnaitno for me to impelemnt MFA.

Problems
* Port Conflict
  - Docker failed to start Nginx becuase port 80 was already in use.
  - Identified the conflict as my pi hole something and remapped the proxy ports.
*502 Error
  - Initial proxy configuraiton returned 502 errors.
  - Identifed the issue as a mispelling in my proxy settings.
  - Corrected spelling and all was swell.
* SSL Cert
  - Had to manually create a certificate
  - Generated a self signed cert using openSSL.
  - Donwloaded the key and certifacte as a file, and uploaded to NPM.

Learned
* How reversee proxies route traffic based on domain name and upstream container name. 
* How TLS termination works at the proxy layer.
* How Docker container networking allows internal name based communicaton. 
* How to identfiy and resolve port bindindg conflicts in a Linux evnrionemnt.
* The differnece between host level ports and contatiner level prots. 
